<div class="page">
  <h2 style="text-align: center;">Dr. Gordon Nelson Searches in China</h2>
  <div class="two-column">
    <div class="column">
      <p>During the month of {{ month_8 }}, JSU Professor J. Gordon Nelson was invited by the International Center at Wuhan University, Hubei Province, China, to participate in the JSU-Wuhan Exchange Program. This unique program was initiated by Professor of Sociology, Dr. Adrian Aveni, who, along with his family, spent two years as a Fulbright Scholar at Wuhan University during 1991-93. Dr. Nelson was invited to participate in the program in {{ year_8 }} to conduct research in the School of Education and Deputy Director of Education Research Evaluation Center.</p>
      <p>Dr. Nelson taught classes in educational technology and research methods to the Wuhan University faculty and students on "Western Higher Education: Trends, Options, and Challenges." He also participated in a series of workshops with the Wuhan faculty and students were 1) the Distance Learning Program developed by Dr. Franklin King and Dr. {{ name_52 }}. 2) The use of LiveTex™ Portfolios developed by Dr. Donna Herring, and 3) the use of an instructional technology in the classroom. The program was an evaluation rubric for tenure and promotion developed by Dr. {{ name_53 }}. "Jacksonville Night at Wuhan" was held on May {{ day_9 }} at a campus restaurant to honor Dr. and Mrs. Nelson. Almost all the Wuhan professors who have come to JSU</p>
    </div>
    <div class="column">
      <p>in the past were present at this occasion. Since the Wuhan professors had been invited several times to the Nelson's home for dinner while at JSU, a happy reunion, Dr. Nelson and his wife were able to spend a wonderful time with the professors from different departments and disciplines. The professors were delighted to meet Dr. Nelson and his wife, and friendships were renewed and new friendships were made. The professors were able to share experiences and learn from each other in the JSU-Wuhan Exchange Program, which has been an invaluable experience for all involved. Dr. Nelson and his wife were asked to bring greetings to friends in Jacksonville. Each professor present wanted to express gratitude for the warm welcome and helpful assistance provided by Dr. Nelson and his wife, and the JSU community. The Wuhan professors were united in their deep appreciation of the memorable time spent and the friendships made while in Jacksonville.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <h2 style="text-align: center;">Goethe Institut Transatlantic Outreach Program</h2>
  <div class="two-column">
    <div class="column">
      <p>Dr. Melinda Odorn Staubs, assistant professor of Elementary Education, was selected to participate in the 2008 Transatlantic Outreach Program (TOP) of the Goethe Institut. TOP is a public/private partnership initiative of the Goethe Institut, Deutsche Bank, and the Robert Bosch Stiftung. TOP selects highly qualified social studies educators in the United States to expose them to every aspect of modern Germany. Dr. Staubs traveled for two weeks this summer through Germany by bus, plane, train, and bicycle, and on foot with a group of sixteen social studies educators from various parts of the United States. Stops throughout Germany provided a variety of experiences and opportunities to learn about the business, history, government, and culture of Germany. The group took part in walking tours of both Berlin and Frankfurt and a bicycle tour throughout the countryside of Dresden. They were able to visit the home of Johann Wolfgang von Goethe, various museums, sail down the</p>
    </div>
    <div class="column">
      <p>Rhine River, and tour a family run restaurant/winery. Some of the activities they participated in were sessions with diplomats of the Federal Foreign Office and German public school curriculum developers, talks with German teachers, and a guided tour of the Reichstag, a tour of the Mercedes Benz museum, a tour of the museum at Sachsenhausen Concentration Camp, and a tour of the information session at the museum of the Holocaust. The group was able to hear firsthand accounts of the effects of the Federal Republic and the rise and fall of the Berlin Wall, take about the complications that developed and remain under discussion.</p>
      <p>Using information garnered from the visit to Germany, Dr. Staubs developed a lesson plan for publication with the Goethe Institut. Through participation in the TOPs program, Dr. Staubs is now a TOP Fellow with the Goethe Institut.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <h2 style="text-align: center;">Best Submission</h2>
  <div class="two-column">
    <div class="column">
      <p>In July 2008, the World Leadership Forum presented Jacksonville State University's Dr. {{ name_54 }} as the Best Submission award, this in conjunction with the Aerospace Journalist of the Year award. Dr. {{ name_54 }} has won such recognition. He also won in 2000, Mr. Chandler's winning entry dealt with the development of cutting-edge aviation maintenance techniques and procedures.</p>
      <p>Mr. Chandler wrote the story for Overhaul & Maintenance magazine, a member of the Aviation Week Group, and a publication of McGraw-Hill.</p>
      <p>The JSU teacher is honored by the recognition, but believes the real benefit derived from his writing is being able to incorporate on-going lessons learned in the professional arena into the classroom.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="column">
      <p>Mr. Chandler is a {{ job_title_1 }} year veteran of JSU, and is the author of the best-selling book Fire & Rain, the chronicle of the crash of Delta Flight 191. The book was turned into a television docudrama.</p>
      <p>Mr. Chandler has appeared on NPR's Weekend Edition, MSNBC, CNN, Good Morning America, PBS's Now, CNN, and will soon be featured in two National Geographic Channel programs dealing with aviation safety.</p>
      <div class="image-container">
        <img src="placeholder_image_8.jpg" alt="Mr. Jerry Chandler Photo">
        <p class="caption">Mr. Jerry Chandler</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>


